#VAR(Table, TABLE)
#ADDTBL("../encoding.tbl", Table)
#ACTIVETBL(Table)
#VAR(PTR, CUSTOMPOINTER)
#CREATEPTR(PTR, "LINEAR", 0, 16)
#JMP($00000FE6)

#WRITE(PTR, $00000B9C)
A fire burned bright into the sky.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000BAC)
Scorching red cinders danced around the incinerator.
<LINE>
The flame was fueled by a sailor-style school uniform, along with%Ncountless textbooks.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D52)
On a foothill overlooking the Seto Inland Sea, a girl stood in%Nher home’s garden.
<LINE>
The steam whistles of boats echoed in the distance, and the nearby%Ntrees gently swayed in the refreshing sea breeze.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D62)
In the midst of such a serene scene, the girl, Orihara Kozue, stood still and alone in front of the incinerator, bathing in the somber%Nambience of its cinders.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D72)
The girl’s gaze was locked onto the school uniform and textbooks as they were slowly reduced to ashes, each becoming clad in a crimson%Nglimmer.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D82)
Until only yesterday, they had been *her* school uniform and *her*%Ntextbooks.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D92)
Despite having lit the fire herself, the girl did not move from the spot—perhaps out of a lingering sense of regret.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000DA2)
That, too, lasted only a moment. She suddenly knitted her eyebrows%Nand covered her ears as hard as she could.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E10)
【梢２】
“...Nh...”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E20)
She gasped in agony.
<LINE>
And yet, she did nothing more.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E30)
The girl did not moan, nor did she raise any tearful%Ncomplaints—all she let out was a series of ragged breaths.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E40)
She shook her head repeatedly from side to side, as if to express a fierce, visceral objection.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E50)
And as she did, her strength slowly started to crumble, and she%Ncollapsed to the ground. She fell on her backside with a thud, let%Nher jaw droop as if utterly exhausted, and looked up to the sky with a dazed expression on her face.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E60)
Minutes later, she was still in that exact same position, not having moved a single muscle.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E70)
Until suddenly, she turned around with conviction, as if she had%Nheard someone call out to her.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000ED2)
She looked toward the house’s porch.
<LINE>
The wooden house had been built over 50 years ago, so despite it%Nbeing daytime, it appeared quite dim. Anything beyond the porch%Ncould barely be seen.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000EE2)
Kozue slowly stood back up and walked over to the porch. And at that very moment, her mother appeared with impeccable timing.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000EF2)
【梢の母２】
“Oh, there you are, Kozue-chan. I’ve been looking all over for you. Have you finished packing yet?”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F02)
Kozue promptly nodded and slowly sat down.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F12)
【梢の母】
“We’ll have to say goodbye to our little ol’ place in a couple of%Ndays.”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F22)
Her mother took a seat next to her, gazing toward the Inland Sea%Nwith a gentle look in her eye.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F32)
【梢の母】
“This will be your first time in Tokyo, won’t it, Kozue-chan?”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F42)
【梢の母】
“Don’t worry... I’m sure you’ll make lots and lots of friends.”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F52)
【梢】
“...”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F62)
Despite her mother’s caring words, Kozue did not nod, nor even shake her head in response.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F72)
She simply remained silent, gazing wistfully toward the%Nstill-burning incinerator in the corner of the garden...
<END>
